course: dictionary
lesson: 5667
title: Little, with many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One small word, little, can have several meanings. Read the words around it to find out which one fits. Ask yourself: is this about size, age, or an amount?
explain: «Your little sister's» tells you little means young, because she is younger than you. «Very little oxygen» means not much oxygen, almost none. «A little milk» means some milk, a small amount.
explain: As an adverb, «a little tired» means slightly tired. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «a little» can be swapped for «slightly» and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Little means small in size: «a little box» is a small box.
fact: Little means young: «my little brother» is my younger brother.
fact: «A little» means some, or a small amount: «a little revision» is a short review.
fact: Little without «a» means not much, almost none: «little time» means there is not much time.
fact: «Little by little» means slowly, bit by bit.
exercise: «I need a little help with my maths.» What does a little mean here? | Quickly | Nothing at all | *Some | Very big
exercise: «Your little sister is only six.» What does little mean? | Tiny | *Young | Rude | Quiet
exercise: «I am a little tired after the match.» What does a little mean? | A small box | Not at all | Very quickly | *Slightly
test: «Arctic foxes have small ears, short legs and short noses that lose little heat.» What does little mean here? | Some, a small amount | *Not much, almost none | Small in size | Young
test: «Here is a little box for your pencils.» What does little mean? | Some | *Small in size | Slightly | Young
exam: «Little by little, the snow melted.» What does little by little mean? | Very small and cold | Quickly, all at once | Young and bright | *Slowly, bit by bit
